# said-vs-done

node_modules/

# Secrets. The tool reads a project's client-facing text, and the projects it
# is pointed at are real ones — an .env picked up from a scanned repository has
# no business coming back here.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Run snapshots. `--json` writes one per run; they hold the full text of every
# promise found in someone's project, so they stay out of the history by
# default. A baseline you deliberately want to keep goes in with `git add -f`.
#
# THE CONFIG FILE IS NOT A SNAPSHOT. `*.said-vs-done.json` also matched
# `.said-vs-done.json` itself — in gitignore `*` matches the empty string — so
# this project's own configuration was silently uncommittable, and the only
# symptom was `git add` refusing a file nobody thought to check on.
.said-vs-done/
!.said-vs-done.json
*-said-vs-done.json
said-vs-done-run*.json

# Editors and OS litter
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store
